fix(pip): Publish each view's FOV, and derive the position decompression from the projection

The systematic sweep for "buffers and parameters a lens pass shader can read but the lens pass
never produces" turned up the real root of the aperture mask, and it is a single quantity.

Device.fFOV is the GAME camera's FOV, and the lens pass keeps the optic's projection in
Device.mProject without touching it. So every FOV derived value in the lens pass described the main
view while the depth buffer had been rendered with a 2.7 degree optic:

 - pos_decompression_params (r4.cpp): the constant every screen space effect uses to turn depth into
   world position. Reconstructed with 65 degrees against a 2.7 degree depth, the fog landed as a
   flat wall across part of the aperture, and it grew with the magnification because the error is
   the ratio of the two FOVs - the mask as reported. It is now derived from the ACTIVE projection
   matrix (-1/_22 and -1/_11, which equal -tan(fov/2) and -tan(fov/2)/aspect, so the main view is
   bit for bit unchanged);
 - the SSAO noise and kernel scale (phase_combine, phase_ssao);
 - the rain volume projection (r4_R_rain);
 - the light visibility cone (light_vis);
 - the light LOD factor (R_calculate).

Rather than patch five sites, svpcam::View now carries the vertical FOV of the view it describes
(captured from Device.fFOV for the main view, set to the smoothed optic FOV by DeriveZoom) and
Apply publishes it, so Device.fFOV follows the view being drawn for the whole frame. The game only
reads Device.fFOV outside Render(), so inside the frame it belongs to the pass. That makes all five
derive the lens camera's FOV, and it costs nothing for the main view, whose value is what it always
was.

This also corrects two earlier statements of mine: the fog's geometry was NOT per pass - it read the
main view's FOV - and the two previous commits (gating the fog and clearing the AO/IL) removed
symptoms of this same cause. Both remain in place: those passes are still unnecessary work for the
lens, and the fog's blend still consumes a blur chain the lens never generates.